data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout cancellation
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running, accept a token (preferred):

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

The caller's `work` delegate checks the token at regular intervals:

```csharp
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `work` is a single blocking call (e.g., `Thread.Sleep(60000)`), it won't stop until it finishes. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers. Always `using`. |
| **Forgetting to pass token through call chain** | If `work` calls sub-methods that also block, each must accept and check the token. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If you cannot modify `work` to check a token (e.g., third-party library), the work will run to completion even after timeout. `Task.WaitAsync` lets your *caller* stop waiting, but the background work continues. For truly uncooperative code, move it to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()`. |

### What was removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` (via constructor timeout) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely — no equivalent needed |
| Manual `new Thread(...)` | `Task.Run` + `WaitAsync` |

### References

- [Thread.Abort breaking change](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/core/compatibility/core-libraries/6.0/thread-abort)
- [Cancellation in managed threads](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/threading/cancellation-in-managed-threads)
- [`Task.WaitAsync`](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.threading.tasks.task.waitasync) (.NET 6+)
